🗓️ WEEK 3 — IMAGE AND VIDEO INPUT PROCESSING

Date: Monday, 31 August 2026

🎯 Objective

The objective of Week 3 was to understand how source images and driving videos are prepared before being passed to the model.

🛠️ Work Completed
Studied the resize() function.
Examined conversion of source images to RGB.
Examined center cropping to a square.
Studied video frame extraction using ImageIO and FFmpeg.
Checked how video FPS is obtained.
Examined conversion of video frames to 256 × 256.
Reviewed the requirement that source images and driving videos should be appropriately cropped before animation.

The supplied implementation crops the image to a square and resizes it to 256 × 256.

⚠️ Error / Problem Faced

An input image that is not square or has an unsupported color mode can create unexpected dimensions or processing problems.

Video files can also fail to decode when FFmpeg support is unavailable or when the video format is incompatible.

✅ Resolution
Converted source images to RGB.
Applied square cropping.
Resized images to the required dimensions.
Installed the required FFmpeg-related packages.
Reviewed the video frame extraction process.
🖼️ Dataset / Project Picture

VoxCeleb examples showing how facial appearance from a source image can be animated using motion extracted from a driving sequence.

💡 Learning

I learned that consistent image dimensions are important because the model configuration expects a 256 × 256 × 3 input.

I also learned that preprocessing is an important part of the machine-learning pipeline because incorrect input dimensions can affect later stages.

